+# Feature Specification: JSON Pointer Resolution (RFC 6901)
+
+**Feature Branch**: `001-json-pointer-resolve`
+**Created**: 2026-10-22
+**Status**: Draft
+**Input**: User description: "Implement JSON Pointer (RFC 6901): export resolvePointer(document, pointer) from src/index.ts; returns the referenced value; throws on invalid pointer syntax or when the pointer does not resolve to a value in the document. Foundation for later features."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Look up a value by pointer (Priority: P1)
+
+A service developer holds a parsed JSON document and a pointer string and wants the value it designates.
+
+**Why this priority**: Core purpose; later features build on it.
+
+**Independent Test**: Resolve the RFC 6901 section 5 example document with every pointer listed there and compare with the RFC's results.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** any document, **When** the pointer is the empty string, **Then** the whole document is returned.
+2. **Given** `{"foo":["bar","baz"]}`, **When** the pointer is `/foo/1`, **Then** `"baz"` is returned.
+3. **Given** keys `a/b`, `m~n`, and the empty key, **When** pointers `/a~1b`, `/m~0n`, `/` are used, **Then** the matching values are returned.
+4. **Given** a member whose value is `null`, `false`, `0` or `""`, **When** its pointer is resolved, **Then** that value is returned (it exists).
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - Reject invalid pointers (Priority: P1)
+
+**Why this priority**: Callers must be able to tell a malformed reference from a valid one.
+
+**Independent Test**: Pass malformed strings and check an error is thrown.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a non-empty pointer not starting with `/` (e.g. `foo`), **Then** an error is thrown.
+2. **Given** a pointer with `~` not followed by `0` or `1` (e.g. `/a~`, `/a~2`), **Then** an error is thrown.
+3. **Given** a pointer that is not a string, **Then** an error is thrown.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - Reject pointers that do not resolve (Priority: P1)
+
+**Independent Test**: Resolve pointers to absent locations and check an error is thrown.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** an object, **When** the pointer names a missing member, **Then** an error is thrown.
+2. **Given** an array, **When** the token is out of range, is `-` (the nonexistent element after the last), is not a valid index (leading zeros such as `01`, signs, non-digits), **Then** an error is thrown.
+3. **Given** a pointer continuing through a primitive or `null` (including strings), **Then** an error is thrown.
+4. **Given** names inherited from JavaScript objects (`__proto__`, `constructor`, `toString`) or array `length`, **When** not own members of the document, **Then** an error is thrown.
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- A pointer `/` against an object lacking the empty key throws.
+- Array index tokens are decimal with no leading zeros (`0` is valid).
+- Duplicate-free objects assumed; the document is never modified.
+- A document member literally named `__proto__` (own property) resolves normally.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` MUST be exported from `src/index.ts` and return the referenced value.
+- **FR-002**: The empty pointer MUST return the whole document.
+- **FR-003**: Pointers MUST be split on `/`; each reference token unescaped by `~1`→`/` then `~0`→`~`.
+- **FR-004**: Non-string pointers, non-empty pointers not beginning with `/`, and invalid `~` escapes MUST throw.
+- **FR-005**: Object tokens MUST match own members only.
+- **FR-006**: Array tokens MUST be `0` or digits without leading zero, and within range; `-` MUST throw.
+- **FR-007**: Traversing into a primitive or `null` MUST throw.
+- **FR-008**: Existing values of any JSON type, including `null`, MUST be returned and never confused with absence.
+- **FR-009**: The document MUST NOT be mutated.
+
+### Key Entities
+
+- **Document**: any parsed JSON value.
+- **Pointer**: string of zero or more `/`-prefixed reference tokens.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+- **SC-001**: All examples of RFC 6901 section 5 produce the RFC's stated results.
+- **SC-002**: 100% of malformed or unresolvable pointer cases listed above throw.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- URI fragment representation (`#/...`) is out of scope; only the string representation is supported.
+- Writing/updating via pointers is out of scope (later features).
+- Error type is a standard `Error` (subclass allowed); messages are not part of the contract.
